ird floats? No, the logic is purely string-based.\n\nHmm, what about value = \"0.0\" with... no.\n\nOK here's another thought: what about `value = \"0\"`? count('.') == 0. num = float(\"0\") = 0.0. value[-2:] = \"0\" \u2260 '.5'. len(\"0\") = 1 > 0 \u2192 res = 0. Line 17.\n\nWhat about value = \".5\"? count('.') == 1. value[-1] = '5' \u2260 '0'. num = 0.5. value[-2:] = \".5\" \u2192 True. num > 0 \u2192 res = ceil(0.5) = 1.\n\nWhat about value = \"-.5\"? count('.') == 1. value[-1] = '5'. num = -0.5. value[-2:] = \".5\" \u2192 True. num > 0 False \u2192 res = floor(-0.5) = -1.\n\nOK so the reachable branches:\n- Line 5 True: any value with exactly one '.', e.g., \"15.5\"\n- Line 5 False: e.g., \"10\"\n- Line 7 True (enter loop): \"10.0\" or \"10.00\"\n- Line 7 False (loop condition fails immediately or after iterations): covered by any case\n- Line 8: loop body\n- Line 11 True: \"15.5\" (positive) and \"-15.5\" (negative)\n- Line 12 True: \"15.5\" \u2192 16\n- Line 12 False: \"-15.5\" \u2192 -16\n- Line 16 True: \"10\" \u2192 10\n- Line 16 False \u2192 line 19: unreachable\n- Line 17: \"10\" \u2192 10\n\nHmm, also need to consider: line 11 `value[-2:] == '.5'` \u2014 what if len(value) == 1? Then value[-2:] is the whole string, e.g., \"5\"[-2:] = \"5\" \u2260 '.5'. Fine.\n\nAlso, what about a value like \"2.5\" vs \"2.50\"? \"2.50\" \u2192 count('.') == 1 \u2192 while: \"2.50\"[-1]='0' \u2192 \"2.5\" \u2192 '5' stop. num = 2.5. value[-2:] = \".5\" \u2192 ceil(2.5) = 3. Good \u2014 this tests the trailing-zero removal leading to '.5' branch.\n\nAnd \"2.500\" \u2192 \"2.5\" \u2192 same.\n\nWhat about \"2.560\"? \u2192 \"2.56\" \u2192 num = 2.56, value[-2:] = \"56\" \u2260 '.5' \u2192 res = int(round(2.56)) = 3. Hmm, round(2.56) = 3. Yes.\n\nBanker's rounding: round(2.5) = 2 in Python 3! But \"2.5\" goes to the '.5' branch, so round is only called for non-.5 values. E.g., round(2.6) = 3, round(2.4) = 2. What about \"2.50\"? \u2192 becomes \"2.5\" \u2192 '.5' branch. OK